      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so my monstera deliciosa has been looking kinda rough lately. some of the lower leaves are turning a weird yellowish color and they feel a bit soft. i think i've been watering it about once a week but i'm not sure if that's too much or if it just needs more food? any idea what's going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, your Monstera deliciosa is showing signs of distress, and it sounds like it might be suffering from overwatering. Monstera plants are quite sensitive to their water levels, and too much of it can lead to root rot, which is what your plant is experiencing. The yellowish leaves and softness are classic signs of this. It's a bit like when a little too much rain floods a cozy cave, and the delicate balance of the cave's ecosystem is thrown off. You might want to reduce your watering frequency and ensure the pot has good drainage. Think of it as giving your plant a chance to breathe and not drowning it in a swamp. And remember, Monstera plants prefer their soil to dry out a bit between waterings, so you might need to adjust your schedule. Keep an eye on it, and it should start to perk up soon. Just like a tired old wizard, it needs a bit of rest and the right conditions to regain its strength.",
